A||||10||Fret Naught||Door Hinge Pins||||5||
Z||000000||Fret Naught||08-05-2000||11:57 PM||||Can anybody give me a heads up on Fiero Door hinge pins? are they notorious for wearing out the bushings? hard to change? I just noticed in the last week or so that my door is scrapping when I open and close it. Unless I lift up on it a little when I close it then it doesn't. I am thinking bushings what do you think?<BR>8511095 <BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||terryk||08-06-2000||01:03 AM||||The bushings and pins should be replaced together. Normal wear. Cheap. <P>The problem is that pesky spring. It is nearly impossible to get back in without the special tool. <P>A friend made one from the picture in the manual. The spring is compressed and locked in the tool. You install it in place and remove the bolt. The tool falls out and the spring is left in place.<P>If anyone has figured out another method for installing the spring, I'd certainly love to hear it. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||08-06-2000||01:47 AM||||So would I as I need to do both doors soon.||||reg||1
Z||000003||My7Fieros||08-06-2000||01:51 AM||||Ditto.......<BR>I got the bushings from a Pontiac dealership, the parts guy said "just take them, you practically keep us in business anyway, haha".......so yeah, Id like to know how to do it to, my drivers door sags BAD!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Gokart||08-06-2000||06:02 AM||||My spring fell out on me when I replaced the pins last Fall. Took a while but eventually got it back in holding it where it's supposed to go with vice grips then pushing hard on one end at a time with a large flat head screw driver till it went back in place. Took a while and at the time I didn't even know that there was actually a tool used for this!||||reg||3
Z||000005||Brian||08-06-2000||07:36 AM||||You might want to take a peek at  <A HREF="http://members.home.net/bmcf/Fiero/HingePins.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.home.net/bmcf/Fiero/HingePins.htm</A> <P>This is more or less how I did the job without a tool for the spring.<P>Good luck,<BR>Brian||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fret Naught||08-06-2000||12:47 PM||||Great advice Brian, Thanks one more thing to do, I will get on that one as soon as I get back from vacation<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FretNaught_sig_3.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||08-06-2000||12:51 PM||||Good idea.  I'll have to figure out what this tool is though, because I don't have access to some of the stuff you used.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||08-06-2000||03:59 PM||||Read my article in the cave as well. I added the nylon washer to mine and it helps considerably. In addition to resoring proper clearance in the lower hinge. It also protects the top bushing in the hinges by off loading the vertical axis to a much bigger surface.<P>The pin sets are available in any parts store in Motormite's red HELP! packages. It's the short GM pin set. You need 2 sets per door.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||terryk||08-06-2000||08:47 PM||||Pretty clever idea there Theo-gre. I'll try that when I change the pins in the Fiero's.<P>Some details are on your site?<P><BR>Terry||||reg||14
Z||000010||theogre||08-07-2000||03:27 PM||||They are on this site. look at the bottom of the page to find the link.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
